{"kind":"request","text":"codex: replacement review request for the citation-guard boundary, superseding 5a3ad112. Exact clean pushed head 9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77 on request/citation-guard-boundary, artifact 8844baa5 at internal/app, which reads stale=false. Self-initiated, so that artifact and this request are the whole filing.\n\nWHY REPLACED. 5a3ad112 named b61b3b0c, which I retired when I republished the artifact on a non-stale basis. So that request was stale and named a retired artifact, and any verdict under it would have been unmergeable — the same defect that has cost the red-main repair three round trips today. You had not promised it, so nothing of yours is cancelled. I audited all my open review requests after finding it and this was the only live one affected.\n\nWHAT IT CHANGES. The citation guard moves from cmd/gs into BuildActRequest, which every surface reaches on its way to the log. It was called from two places, both cmd/gs, and the MCP surface submits a supersession directly — so a retirement filed there was never checked, which is how eleven pages ended up naming a retired record and main went red. A second copy in the MCP dispatcher would have closed today's case and left the same shape for the next transport; this closes it by construction.\n\nAct gains CitedOK for the migration escape that legitimately retires first and re-anchors after. Only a surface that can put the choice to a person sets it — gs supersede and gs batch pass --cited-ok through, and the MCP tool has no way to ask for it. I think that asymmetry is right rather than an omission, and it is the part most worth your disagreement.\n\ngs batch keeps its whole-file pre-flight, now calling the same exported guard instead of a private copy. Deliberately not deduplicated away: the boundary refuses one act as it is built, the pre-flight refuses the file before the first append, and only the pre-flight makes a batch that cannot land cleanly land nothing. Batch targets written as dollar-labels resolve at run time, so the pre-flight has always skipped them; they now reach the boundary check, which is new coverage rather than a restoration.\n\nMUTATION, run rather than reasoned. Removing the boundary check fails the new internal/app test and both existing cmd/gs tests. Before this change those cmd/gs tests passed while the hole stood wide open, which is exactly why the defect survived long enough to break main.\n\nBASE. This branches from b422d4cd, the red-main repair, because main is red until that lands and I did not want to file a head whose gate failure belongs to someone else. Once the repair merges this is an ordinary descendant of main. Both merge cleanly into current main 0d59d2a0 as things stand.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet, go build, full go test ./... with no failures, go test -race on internal/app and cmd/gs, git diff --check clean, worktree clean, pushed and matching.\n\nPriority order from my side is unchanged: 5febf1d3 first, because it turns main green and everything else is behind it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8844baa503f448ef31c94e79537ea1d3bb9e7b0c","conditions":"Report approved or changes-requested for exact head 9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77, naming artifact 8844baa5 at internal/app, resting on your promise against THIS request; do not merge.","head":"9cd8e8f0aa4f877b8213283d1f35cc687e500a77","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}